values ('531857f0-bbfb-4884-8a67-ced2fcf87fc3', 'discrete-mathematics', 'Discrete Mathematics', 'Discrete Mathematics is one common foundation across Computer Science, Mathematics, and Computer Engineering. Students translate informal claims into precise logical, set-theoretic, relational, graph, and automata models, then prove, count, compute, and communicate those models. The course moves from mathematical language, logic, sets, functions, and proof through combinatorics, discrete probability, number theory, recurrences, graphs, Boolean algebra, automata, computability, and complexity. One rigorous core serves all three degrees. Computer Science emphasizes invariants, algorithm analysis, automata, and security, Mathematics emphasizes bijections, generating functions, and theorem-quality exposition, and Computer Engineering emphasizes Boolean minimization, state machines, coding, and network models.');

